% 46-codegen-tests.weft — Code Generation Tests
%
% End-to-end tests for the ARM64 backend.  Each test is a small C
% program compiled by kcc, linked by the system linker, and executed.
% The test passes if the exit code matches the expected value.
% The shell script is tangled to tmp/tests/test_codegen.sh and run
% by 'just test-codegen'.

\chapter{Code Generation Tests}
\label{ch:codegen-tests}

The lexer conformance tests of Chapter~\ref{ch:conformance-tests} exercise
the compiler's frontend by calling internal functions from ARM64 assembly.
Code generation tests require a different strategy: they compile complete C
programs with \kcc{}, link the resulting object files into executables, and
verify that the process exits with the expected status code.  A shell script
orchestrates the pipeline---write source, compile, link, run, compare---and
reports results in the same pass/fail format as the unit test harness.

Individual test groups are defined alongside the features they exercise:
constants and arithmetic in Chapter~\ref{ch:codegen}, local variables,
control flow, switch statements, ternary expressions, sizeof, arrays,
structs, multi-variable declarations, unsigned operations and type
extensions, increment/decrement, compound
assignment, logical operators, function calls, pointers, and
miscellaneous cases in
Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-builder}.  This chapter provides the runner
infrastructure that ties them together.

\section{Test Runner}

The script defines a helper function \texttt{run\_test} that receives a
test name, a C source string, and the expected exit code.  It writes the
source to a temporary file, invokes \texttt{tmp/kcc~-c} to produce an
object file, links it with the system linker, runs the resulting binary,
and compares the actual exit code against the expectation.  Failures print
the expected and actual values for diagnosis.

A sibling \texttt{run\_test\_include} additionally writes a companion
header \texttt{defs.h} next to the source before compiling, so that
tests can exercise \texttt{\#include "defs.h"} and confirm the
preprocessor resolves a header sitting beside the translation unit.
A second sibling \texttt{run\_test\_multi} writes two source files and
compiles them with a single \texttt{kcc~-c~a.c~b.c} invocation, then
links both objects, exercising multi-translation-unit builds and
cross-object symbol resolution.

@d Test: codegen runner @{
#!/bin/bash
# kcc code generation test suite
# Generated by weft — do not edit
set -u

PASS=0
FAIL=0
TOTAL=0
KCC="tmp/kcc"
TMPDIR="/tmp/kcc_test_$$"
SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path 2>/dev/null)

mkdir -p "$TMPDIR"
trap 'rm -rf "$TMPDIR"' EXIT

run_test() {
    local name="$1"
    local src="$2"
    local expected="$3"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))

    echo "$src" > "$TMPDIR/t.c"

    # Compile
    if ! $KCC -c "$TMPDIR/t.c" -o "$TMPDIR/t.o" 2>/dev/null; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (compile error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi

    # Link
    if ! ld "$TMPDIR/t.o" -o "$TMPDIR/t" \
            -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" \
            -e _main -arch arm64 2>/dev/null; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (link error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi

    # Run
    "$TMPDIR/t"
    local actual=$?

    if [ "$actual" -eq "$expected" ]; then
        PASS=$((PASS + 1))
        printf "  %-40s PASS\n" "$name..."
    else
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (expected %d, got %d)\n" "$name..." "$expected" "$actual"
    fi
}

# Variant for #include: writes a companion header "defs.h" beside the source
# so that the preprocessor's source-relative resolution locates it.
run_test_include() {
    local name="$1"
    local header="$2"
    local src="$3"
    local expected="$4"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))

    echo "$header" > "$TMPDIR/defs.h"
    echo "$src" > "$TMPDIR/t.c"

    if ! $KCC -c "$TMPDIR/t.c" -o "$TMPDIR/t.o" 2>/dev/null; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (compile error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi
    if ! ld "$TMPDIR/t.o" -o "$TMPDIR/t" \
            -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" \
            -e _main -arch arm64 2>/dev/null; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (link error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi

    "$TMPDIR/t"
    local actual=$?

    if [ "$actual" -eq "$expected" ]; then
        PASS=$((PASS + 1))
        printf "  %-40s PASS\n" "$name..."
    else
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (expected %d, got %d)\n" "$name..." "$expected" "$actual"
    fi
}

# Variant for multi-file compilation: compiles two translation units in a
# single "kcc -c a.c b.c" invocation, then links both objects together.
run_test_multi() {
    local name="$1"
    local file_a="$2"
    local file_b="$3"
    local expected="$4"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))

    echo "$file_a" > "$TMPDIR/a.c"
    echo "$file_b" > "$TMPDIR/b.c"

    if ! $KCC -c "$TMPDIR/a.c" "$TMPDIR/b.c" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (compile error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi
    if ! ld "$TMPDIR/a.o" "$TMPDIR/b.o" -o "$TMPDIR/t" \
            -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" \
            -e _main -arch arm64 2>/dev/null; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (link error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi

    "$TMPDIR/t"
    local actual=$?

    if [ "$actual" -eq "$expected" ]; then
        PASS=$((PASS + 1))
        printf "  %-40s PASS\n" "$name..."
    else
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (expected %d, got %d)\n" "$name..." "$expected" "$actual"
    fi
}

echo "kcc codegen test suite"
echo "======================"
@}

\section{Results}

After all test groups have run, the script prints a summary line
and exits with status~0 only if every test passed.

@d Test: codegen results @{
echo ""
echo "======================"
echo "Results: $PASS passed, $FAIL failed (of $TOTAL)"
[ "$FAIL" -eq 0 ]
@}
